  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThere were no significant changes in the forecast reasoning today.\nThere may be some fog potential late tonight into early Friday\nmorning, especially over northeast portions of the area where the\nmost favorable conditions for cooling of the humid boundary layer\nwill exist. Depending on how fog formation goes, there may be \nneed for formal messaging with the graphics, but confidence was \ntoo low at this point.\n\nOverall, the transition away from summer is appearing in the \nsynoptic pattern taking shape over the next week. Upper-level \nridging generally around the southern/central parts of the country\nwill still keep temperatures near to above normal in the broader \nregion, but split flow is noted in the guidance into next week. \nSouthern jet stream around the periphery of the ridge will carry \nembedded shortwaves and add more flow and shear to the atmosphere.\nShortwaves may bring showers or storms around Sunday/Monday, but \nthe models disagree whether ridging or troughing will be more \nestablished to allow much. Either way, the conditions will be \ndrier without concern for humidity causing dangerous heat stress.\n\nBy the time we get to the middle of next week, the ensemble of\nguidance shows a breakdown of the ridge and a more full-latitude \ntrough swinging from the western CONUS toward the central CONUS. \nBeing a bit early in the transitional time of year, confidence on \nthe timing of this features is very low. Official guidance leans \ninto a faster and more progressive low with a cold front and \nstorms in our area by Wednesday. The ECMWF and other guidance \ncontinue to suggest a slower low cutting off around the Desert \nSouthwest and continued warm advection across our forecast area \nand region (rain to the northwest still) through Wednesday. We \nwill definitely have to monitor this potential system, as it marks\na change from the stagnant summer pattern and a potentially more \npotent cool-season pattern. In addition, there will be significant\nsubtropical moisture related to east Pacific tropical cyclone \nactivity. /NF/EC/",
